    "justification": "Every numerical bound now names the exact rule, atoms, weights, analytic reduction, and outward replacement charge needed for reproduction.",
    "gap": "No published paper studies this four-label fan, and the rigorous enclosure width remains \\(0.452699\\), far above the requested milliscale gap.",
    "consumer": "The explicit formulas are mandatory regression objects for every implementation of the global certificate algorithm.",
    "proof_tex": "Put \\(q=49/25\\) and whiten \\(w=(a,b)\\) to \\(z=(a/\\sqrt{1/8},b/\\sqrt{3/20})\\). Every row of every \\(F_v\\) has Euclidean norm one. If \\(h\\in D_v\\), both inward facet means are nonnegative. The union bound gives\n\\[\n P_h\\{v\\notin Q_q(W)\\}\\le2\\Phi(-q).\n\\]\nThe alternating exponential series, integrated on rational intervals and combined with \\(0.3989422804014<(2\\pi)^{-1/2}<0.3989422804015\\), gives\n\\[\n 0.0249978951482<\\Phi(-49/25)<0.0249978951483.\n\\]\nThus \\(2\\Phi(-49/25)<0.0499957902966<0.05\\), proving global honesty, including equality faces.\n\nLet \\(h\\in H_2\\) have unique label \\(v\\). Separation by two implies that both standardized inward facet means are at least two. If both observed inward coordinates exceed \\(q\\), all rivals are excluded, so the loss is zero. The two standardized facet noises have correlation at least \\(\\rho_*=-\\sqrt{3/13}\\). With \\(t=2-q=1/25\\), monotonicity of the Gaussian orthant probability in its correlation gives\n\\[\n P_h\\{Q_q(W)=\\{v\\}\\}\\ge\\Phi_2(t,t;\\rho_*).\n\\]\nDirect differentiation gives\n\\[\n \\frac d{du}\\Phi_2(u,u;\\rho_*)\n =2\\phi(u)\\Phi\\!\\left(u\\sqrt{\\frac{1-\\rho_*}{1+\\rho_*}}\\right)\n \\ge\\phi(u),\\qquad u\\ge0.\n\\]\nThe centered polar integral is\n\\[\n \\Phi_2(0,0;\\rho_*)=\\frac14-\\frac{\\arctan\\sqrt{3/10}}{2\\pi}.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n \\sup_{h\\in H_2}E_h\\ell\\{Q_q(W)\\}\n \\le \\frac34+\\frac{\\arctan\\sqrt{3/10}}{2\\pi}\n -\\{\\Phi(1/25)-1/2\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\nOutward series arithmetic gives\n\\[\n 0.0797514300099<\\frac{\\arctan\\sqrt{3/10}}{2\\pi}<0.0797514300101,\n \\qquad\n 0.0159534368527<\\Phi(1/25)-\\frac12<0.0159534368530.\n\\]\nThe right side of (5) is below \\(0.8137979931574<0.813799\\).\n\nFor the lower certificate, let \\(\\mu_\\pm=(\\pm1/\\sqrt2,2)\\) and \\(\\mu_0=(0,2)\\). The Mahalanobis distance of \\(\\mu_+\\) from the rival across \\(a=0\\) is two and all other rival distances exceed two; symmetry treats \\(\\mu_-\\). Thus both risk atoms lie in \\(H_2\\). In the standardized first coordinate \\(z\\), the risk-mixture density divided by \\(\\phi(z)\\) is \\(e^{-2}\\cosh(2z)\\). For\n\\[\n a_0=1028/625,\\qquad \\lambda_0=e^{-2}\\cosh(2a_0),\n\\]\nthe curves cross at \\(z=\\pm a_0\\). Pointwise minimization over actions reduces the dual integral to the minimum of the risk-mixture density and \\(\\lambda_0\\phi(z)\\), so\n\\[\n D_0=\\Phi(a_0-2)-\\Phi(-a_0-2)\n +\\lambda_0\\{2\\Phi(-a_0)-1/10\\}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\nOutward rational series bounds give\n\\[\n \\begin{gathered}\n 1.8181887545408<\\lambda_0<1.8181887545411,\\\\\n 0.3612198823625<\\Phi(a_0-2)<0.3612198823627,\\\\\n 0.0001337999106<\\Phi(-a_0-2)<0.0001337999108,\\\\\n 0.0500055310938<\\Phi(-a_0)<0.0500055310941.\n \\end{gathered}\n\\]\nHence \\(D_0>0.3611061955936\\). Replacing \\(\\lambda_0\\) by \\(\\lambda_4\\) changes (6), including its penalty, by less than \\(3753/10^{15}\\). Thus \\(L_4>0.3611061955898>0.361100\\). Atomic-dual soundness gives the lower endpoint.\n\nAt \\(r=0\\), honesty at the common contact \\(h=0\\) gives\n\\[\n E_0|K(W)|=\\sum_{v\\in V_4}P_0\\{v\\in K(W)\\}\\ge4(1-1/20)=19/5.\n\\]\nSince \\(|A|\\le1+3\\mathbf1\\{|A|\\ge2\\}\\),\n\\[\n P_0\\{|K(W)|\\ge2\\}\\ge\\frac{19/5-1}{3}=\\frac{14}{15}.\n\\]\nUniquely labelled means approach the contact, and convolution with a Gaussian density makes bounded loss expectations continuous in the mean; hence \\(R_0(K)\\ge14/15\\). The rule \\(K_{\\mathrm{ct}}\\) includes each label with probability \\(14/15+1/60=19/20\\) and incurs loss only when it reports \\(V_4\\), with probability \\(14/15\\). It attains the bound.",
